#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3892" :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$r(t) = 300t^2+50$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour.\n\n1. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n2. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\nTo find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2$, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}300t^{2} + 50dt \\\\\n&= 100t^{3} + 50t \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (100 \\cdot 2^{3} + 50(2)) - (0) \\\\\n&= (800 + 100) \\\\\n&= 900\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\npeople per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t)$, is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}R(t)dt &= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t) - 25dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}r(t)dt_(⏟)_(900) - ∫_{0}^{2}25dt \\\\\n&= 900 - [25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 900 - 50 \\\\\n&= 850\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147277 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3893" :question/problem "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 6t^2+2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" :question/worked-solution "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} + 2 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= T(0) + ∫_{0}^{t}R(x)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= 50 + ∫_{0}^{1}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + [2x^{3} - 4x] \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 50 + [(2(1)^{3} - 4(1)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= 50 - 2 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147278 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3894" :question/problem "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$T(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,$.\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0$, find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(50 + ∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(50) + \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}∫_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}\n$$\n\nand $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0$,\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nmust be the minimum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baeaa4c4-4405-5822-ae92-af3f34c07c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.5$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145736} {:answer/id #uuid "cdee0656-2c5c-5d91-8688-bd283bf6e4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.3$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145737} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd04192-b583-56f5-9fa9-7e9ea7cd0691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.9$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145738} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3c696c-2b23-5271-81a0-9e4c9b603ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.6$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145739} {:answer/id #uuid "57a88f96-83d4-5844-9dfe-e09fccf173cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.2$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdee0656-2c5c-5d91-8688-bd283bf6e4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.3$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145737} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e83019-afe5-512a-972c-d1c252eb5b3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145735}] :question/id #uuid "c80fa1ad-2eb4-57c3-a5fa-15e425ac24ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-62799" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA water tank is filling with water. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 20e^{-2t}$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes since the tank started filling up.\n\nTo one decimal place, how much water enters the tank between $t = 1$ minute and $t = 3$ minutes?" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0bf0f78-1620-5568-9c64-804451f04523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $10000$ people after $0$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145790} {:answer/id #uuid "d63fbcd1-0292-5791-b359-35ea134c04ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $8000$ people after $2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145791} {:answer/id #uuid "dc246e1a-6bfc-5aa8-874e-44e463a8e952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $5000$ people after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145792} {:answer/id #uuid "114ec758-1310-553c-8628-009fdc055153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $4000$ people after $5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145793} {:answer/id #uuid "28fbc621-c8a0-5745-ab2b-62040882d63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $6000$ people after $2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fbc621-c8a0-5745-ab2b-62040882d63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $6000$ people after $2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145794} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa0b017-e385-50e1-8fea-c8ad883a379c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145789}] :question/id #uuid "1a67a0e4-a63a-5fd5-adfe-805643baa3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-62828" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe total number of people $T(t)$ at a festival after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$T(t) = 10 + ∫_{0}^{t}2x - 4dx$,\n\nwhere $T$ is measured in thousands of people, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nAt what time is the number of people at the festival a minimum, and how many people are at the festival at this time?" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
